  }))), "Related Works"), mdx("p", null, "Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting has been shown to significantly improve the ability of large language models to perform complex reasoning by generating explicit intermediate steps before producing an answer ", "[Wei et al., 2022]", ". However, CoT introduces two major types of costs that are especially problematic in multilingual and low-resource settings. First, manual-CoT (few-shot, human-written reasoning demonstrations) require tedious, task-specific annotations that do not easily generalize across new domains or languages ", "[Wei et al., 2022; Zhang et al., 2022]", ". Second, verbose CoT rationales dramatically increase inference cost: outputs can be an order of magnitude longer than direct answers, inflating token usage, latency, and, in multilingual cases, amplifying error propagation through translation and script mixing ", "[Xu et al., 2025; Han et al., 2025]", ".\nTo reduce these costs, prior work has explored several directions.\n(a) Automatic demonstration generation, including Auto-CoT ", "[Zhang et al., 2022]", ", Consistency-based Self-Adaptive Prompting (COSP) ", "[Wan et al., 2023]", ", and Universal Self-Adaptive Prompting (USP) ", "[Wan et al., 2023]", ", eliminates the need for human-authored CoT exemplars by having models generate or refine their own demonstrations.\n(b) Concise reasoning methods, such as Chain-of-Draft (CoD) ", "[Xu et al., 2025]", ", Zero-shot CoT ", "[Tojima et al, 2022]", ", and Token-Budget-Aware LLM Reasoning (TALE) ", "[Han et al., 2025]", ", explicitly aim to compress or constrain rationales to reduce inference cost while preserving accuracy.\n(c) Adaptive computation and uncertainty-aware decoding, including RouteLLM ", "[Ong et al., 2024]", ", Mixture-of-Experts gating strategies ", "[Li et al., 2023]", ", and Contrastive Decoding variants ", "[Li et al., 2023]", ", seek to minimize compute by selectively allocating reasoning effort or stabilizing token choices through contrastive expert - base model reweighting."), mdx("p", null, "Despite this progress, very little of this literature examines CoT in multilingual or low-resource languages, and even less addresses the unique challenges these languages pose. Moreover, existing efficient CoT methods assume high-resource training conditions and well-calibrated reasoning behavior, which do not hold for low-resource languages like Nepali."), mdx("p", null, "This project directly addresses this gap by evaluating whether a substantially shorter reasoning cue, Minimal-CoT (\u201CLet\u2019s think briefly.\u201D) can recover the benefits of standard step-by-step CoT while avoiding these costs. By comparing No-CoT, Minimal-CoT, and Standard-CoT across Nepali and English reasoning benchmarks , we test whether concise reasoning triggers can offer a more efficient, more robust, and more cross-lingually stable alternative to verbose CoT in low-resource settings."), mdx("h2", {
